TELNETD(8)          UNIX Programmer's Manual          TELNETD(8)


NAME
     telnetd - DARPA TELNET protocol server

SYNOPSIS
     telnetd [port]

DESCRIPTION
     Telnetd is a server which supports the DARPA standard TELNET
     virtual terminal protocol.  It is 2.11BSD's telnetd(8).
     Inetd(8) starts it for each connection to the telnet port,
     with the connection as its standard input, output and error;
     see /etc/inetd.conf.

     Given a port, telnetd instead listens on that port itself,
     on every address, serves the first connection and exits.

     Telnetd allocates a pseudo-terminal, see pty(4), and creates
     a child with the slave side as its standard input, output
     and error.  The child is login(1), run as `login -h host -p'
     with the client's host name, or its address when it has no
     name.  Telnetd holds the master side
     and passes data between it and the connection, taking the
     TELNET commands out of the client's stream and doubling a
     data byte of 255 on the way back.

     When a session starts telnetd sends WILL ECHO and WILL
     SUPPRESS GO AHEAD, and asks the client for DO ECHO (to
     recognize a 4.2BSD client), DO TERMINAL TYPE and DO NAWS.
     It answers a request for BINARY and for TIMING MARK, and
     refuses every other option.

     The terminal type is asked for and waited for before the
     child is created, and is given to login(1) as TERM in its
     environment.
     The window size, when the client sends one, is set on the
     pseudo-terminal, and a later change is signalled to the
     session as SIGWINCH.

     The TELNET commands are acted on as follows.  Interrupt
     Process and Break put the terminal's interrupt and quit
     characters into the session's input.  Abort Output puts in
     its flush character, discards output not yet sent and sends
     a Data Mark as urgent data.  Erase Character and Erase Line
     put in the erase and kill characters.  Are You There is
     answered with [Yes].

     When the child exits telnetd clears the session's utmp(5)
     entry, adds the logout to wtmp, restores the modes and owner
     of the pseudo-terminal and closes the connection.

FILES
     /testbin/telnetd    the server; see BUGS
     /bin/login          run on the slave side
     /dev/pty[p-s]?      master sides, tried in order
     /dev/tty[p-s]?      slave sides
     /etc/utmp, /etc/wtmp

SEE ALSO
     telnet(1), vhangup(2), inetd(8), login(1), pty(4)

BUGS
     These differ from 2.11BSD.

     The banner names FGX.

     The window size option is an addition; 2.11BSD's telnetd
     does not know it.  With it come two things that telnetd does
     not have: each suboption is read from an empty buffer, as in
     4.3BSD-Reno's telnetd, and SIGTTOU is ignored, as in rlogind.

     The listener that 2.11BSD compiles in under DEBUG is chosen
     here by giving a port.

     Some TELNET commands are only partially implemented, and the
     terminal type name is truncated to 40 characters, as in
     2.11BSD.

     Telnetd is staged as /testbin/telnetd, which is not on the
     default path.



